+# Specialty Directory Derived in Memory Rather Than Mapped as an Inverse Association
+
+**Status:** Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+The veterinarian-to-specialty association is mapped in one direction only, from `Vet`. The specialty directory asks the inverse question, and a specialty no veterinarian holds must still appear on the page.
+
+Three facts constrain the answer. Open sessions are not held across view rendering, so any association a view reads is fetched eagerly. `VetRepository.findAll()` results are cached and shared across requests. `Vet` is also serialized on the machine-readable vet route.
+
+## Options Considered
+
+1. **Inverse `@ManyToMany(mappedBy)` on `Specialty`** — a bidirectional association. It needs eager fetch to survive the closed session, and it makes the serialized vet representation cyclic.
+2. **A JPQL entity join returning specialty–veterinarian pairs** — one query, but a query shape with no precedent in this codebase, and the caller still regroups the rows.
+3. **Read both collections and pair them in memory** — two queries, with the pairing owned by one type.
+
+## Decision
+
+We take option 3. `SpecialtyRepository` reads the specialties in name order, `VetRepository.findAll()` supplies the veterinarians, and `SpecialtyDirectory` pairs them.
+
+`SpecialtyDirectory` is an immutable per-request projection, not a value object from the pattern catalog. Its components are persistence entities carrying identity, so value equality is unavailable to it. It realizes the catalog's other value-object properties: immutability, no identity of its own, and invariants enforced at construction.
+
+Pairing matches on entity identity. The persisted types define no value equality, and the veterinarians arrive from the cache while the specialties arrive from a fresh read, so the two sides are distinct instances of the same rows.
+
+The projection copies before ordering, because sorting the collection returned by the cached read in place would corrupt the cached entry.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The persisted model is unchanged, and the serialized vet representation stays acyclic.
+- The pairing is unit-testable with real `Vet` and `Specialty` instances, which keeps the rule out of the controller.
+- A specialty carrying no stored identity is rejected at construction. Without the guard, two absent ids compare equal and an unsaved specialty pairs with a veterinarian's unsaved one.
+- Ordering and the held-by-none case live in one type rather than in the template.
+- Rendering the page reads the whole veterinarian collection. That is acceptable at this clinic's scale; a filtered query is the answer if the roster grows.
+- Specialty reads are uncached, so the unevicted-cache question recorded in the system design does not widen.
+
+## Implementation
+
+**Requirements:** REQ-SPECIALTYDIRECTORY-001
+
+## References
+
+- [Contracts](../system-design.md#contracts) — the types realizing this decision
+- [Open Questions from the Survey](../system-design.md#open-questions-from-the-survey) — item 5, vet cache invalidation
